# interface Shape { area: number; sides: number }  const circle: Shape = { area: 12.5, sides: 0 }  const square = { area: 4 } as Shape  function areaOf(value: any) { return (value as Shape).area }  register(areaOf, circle, square)
# The object part of a type is a family: the layouts that the program converts to the type, and those that have the names it requires. An object with a proven type has one of the layouts that the type lists, and typed code finds its slots by that list.
# The lie: `supertypes=[L0]` is gone from Square, as if the front end had missed the assertion that takes a Square for a Shape. A Square has no `sides`, so its names do not put it there either, and the family of Shape lists too few layouts.
# Where a test stands that costs speed and nothing else: the test fails and the generic code goes on. A family does its harm where nothing tests, so the first type test of every function passes whatever it is given (drop-guard).
# In `areaOf` that is the test of the conversion, and behind it a Square stands where the type lists Shape and Circle.
# A validating build tests the value that the passing edge hands on against the layouts of its type, not only for being an object. It finds the Square and ends the process.
# With the Circle the same build runs to its end: the dropped test would have passed
